WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.320
Why would a polymer fall apart while its building blocks crowd around it?

2
00:00:05.380 --> 00:00:09.120
Watch one microtubule grow from a seed in purified tubulin.

3
00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:14.340
It lengthens slowly for minutes, then collapses many times faster.

4
00:00:15.560 --> 00:00:18.060
A crystal in its solution does not do this.

5
00:00:21.467 --> 00:00:24.127
Start with one end, and treat it as a doorway.

6
00:00:25.727 --> 00:00:28.267
Subunits arrive at a rate set by how many are free.

7
00:00:29.387 --> 00:00:32.207
They leave at a rate that does not depend on that.

8
00:00:33.367 --> 00:00:35.927
At one free concentration, the two balance.

9
00:00:37.167 --> 00:00:39.247
That is the end's critical concentration,

10
00:00:40.467 --> 00:00:41.967
Above it, the end grows.

11
00:00:43.547 --> 00:00:44.547
Below it, the end shrinks.

12
00:00:45.827 --> 00:00:50.667
If subunits never used their nucleotide, both ends would share one critical

13
00:00:50.667 --> 00:00:56.847
concentration. The polymer would grow until free subunits fell to that level, then

14
00:00:56.847 --> 00:00:57.847
simply settle.

15
00:00:58.700 --> 00:01:00.840
Real actin and tubulin differ.

16
00:01:02.180 --> 00:01:05.680
Each subunit arrives carrying fresh ATP or GTP.

17
00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:12.200
Some time after joining, it hydrolyses that nucleotide and releases the phosphate.

18
00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.020
Those old subunits then hold on less tightly.

19
00:01:17.083 --> 00:01:18.603
Here is the whole idea.

20
00:01:20.063 --> 00:01:23.683
Hydrolysis after joining gives one polymer two kinds of end.

21
00:01:24.843 --> 00:01:29.063
A tight end shows fresh subunits and has a low critical concentration.

22
00:01:30.543 --> 00:01:33.523
A loose end shows old subunits and has a high one.

23
00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:39.573
So set the free subunits between the two levels: above the tight end's, below the

24
00:01:39.573 --> 00:01:42.753
loose end's. What can the polymer do now?

25
00:01:48.267 --> 00:01:49.827
It cannot come to rest.

26
00:01:51.187 --> 00:01:52.327
Actin answers in space.

27
00:01:53.707 --> 00:01:58.287
Its barbed end adds subunits fast and stays fresh: a tight end that grows.

28
00:01:59.707 --> 00:02:03.447
Its pointed end shows old subunits: a loose end that shrinks.

29
00:02:04.727 --> 00:02:08.467
When gain and loss match, the length holds while subunits flow through.

30
00:02:09.627 --> 00:02:10.627
That is treadmilling.

31
00:02:11.717 --> 00:02:14.197
A microtubule answers in time instead.

32
00:02:15.517 --> 00:02:20.297
While growing, it wears a cap of fresh GTP-tubulin, so its end stays tight.

33
00:02:21.657 --> 00:02:25.617
Behind the cap, GDP-tubulin is held straight only by its neighbours.

34
00:02:26.850 --> 00:02:31.870
The cap can be lost by chance, or when growth slows and hydrolysis catches up.

35
00:02:33.070 --> 00:02:34.230
Then the end turns loose.

36
00:02:35.570 --> 00:02:40.210
Its protofilaments peel outward, and it shortens many times faster than it grew.

37
00:02:41.450 --> 00:02:42.870
That switch is a catastrophe.

38
00:02:44.470 --> 00:02:46.110
Growth starting again is a rescue.

39
00:02:47.233 --> 00:02:52.553
Exactly what commits an end to collapse is still argued, and in cells other proteins

40
00:02:52.553 --> 00:02:58.633
tune the rates. Hydrolysis gives tight and loose ends, so the polymer cannot simply

41
00:02:58.633 --> 00:03:03.773
settle. Cells use that restlessness to search their interior and rebuild.
